        "heading": "4.1 EXPERIMENTAL SETUP",
        "text": "We conduct our experiments on Cifar-10 and Cifar-100 (Krizhevsky & Hinton (2009)). For selfsupervised learning model we use ResNet-18 (He et al. (2016)) as the encoder. Following the original setting of BYOL, we use 2-layer MLPs as the projection and prediction heads. We use terms \u2019representations\u2019 or \u2019embeddings\u2019 interchangeably as the output of the encoder. The representations are used for downstream tasks. We use the same set of data augmentations in Grill et al. (2020) on both Cifar-10 and Cifar-100, consists of random cropping and resizing with a random horizontal flip, followed by a color distortion and a grayscale conversion.\nFor cSGHMC, we place a N (0, I) prior on the parameters of the online network and use a cosine decay learning rate schedule (Zhang et al. (2020)) with cycle length of 50. We use momentum term of 0.9 and a batch size of 64. For pretraining on Cifar-10 we use initial learning rate \u2113 = 0.5 and inject Gaussian noise at epoch 40. For pretraining on Cifar-100 we use initial learning rate \u2113 = 0.4 and inject Gaussian noise at epoch 47. We collect 3 samples at the end of each cycle. We pretrain models on 95% of training data of Cifar-10 and Cifar-100 without labels, leaving 5% as validation set to monitore overfitting. In cSGHMC it is important to scale the noise and prior with the length of the dataset (Florian et al. (2020)).\nWe compare with a self-supervised model trained with SGD optimizer. For the SGD baseline, we use SGD with the same momentum term. We use fixed learning rate schedule with \u2113 = 0.03 and the same batch size. We do not use any weight decay. We pretrained all models on Cifar-10 and Cifar-100 for the same number of epochs 1500 and unless otherwise stated we take parameter samples starting from epoch 1200 resulting in 21 samples in Cifar-10 and from epoch 1300 resulting\nin 15 samples in Cifar-100. We refer to BYOL pretrained with cSGHMC as BBYOL, and its deterministic counterpart pretrained with SGD as BYOL. We also take samples in MAP optimization during pertaining at the same epochs as in cSGHMC and take marginalizing over these parameters as another baseline. If we marginalize over the representations in downstream task, we use terms BBYOL-ENS and BYOL-ENS respectively."
