        "heading": "4.2 SEMI-SUPERVISED EVALUATION",
        "text": "One of the common ways to evaluate the quality of representations learnt via a self-supervised learning model is in semi-supervised learning task. In this task a pretrained model is fine-tuned on subsets of original training dataset with labels. We use subsets of size 1%, 10%, 50% and 100% of Cifar-10 labeled training data. Table 1 indicates results obtained on the Cifar-10 test set averaged over 4 runs. First we observe that representations obtained from BBYOL improve performance in all subsets of labeled data. The improvement is higher when we fine tun on larger portion of data (e.g. 50% and 100%). The second observation is that marginalizing over the representations in BYOL-ENS also improves performance specially in low data regime. It is due to the nature of contrastive loss which induces diversity in the parameter space. Whenever the loss is not too high, marginalizing over these representations improve the performance. Further, BBYOL-ENS significantly outperforms other baselines, when we fine tune on 100% of labeled data. In order to investigate the generalizability of our findings, we also provide the results obtained on the Cifar-100 test set in Table 2. We fine-tune a pretarined model on Cifar-100 on the splits of 10%, 50% and 100% of training data with labels and report results on the test set averaged over 3 runs. Consistent with our previous results, marginalizing over representations improve performance in both BYOLENS and BBYOL-ENS with a better performance for BBYOL-ENS when fine-tuned on larger splits of data. We should highlight this point that results on Cifar-100 (and also Cifar-10) can be improved if we use a larger architecture, but our goal here is not benchmarking against other self-supervised learning models, rather we want to show the effectiveness of our proposed method compared to the baselines which were pretrained with the same architecture and setting."
